00:15:49.995 --> 00:15:51.870
Usually, when I find
something that I'm going

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:55.450
to actually need, I print it.

00:15:55.450 --> 00:15:59.080
And this is a rail stack
that I'm working with.

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:01.536
And this is fine.

00:16:01.536 --> 00:16:03.430
[END PLAYBACK]

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:05.220
LAURA BANDURA: So
as Anne suggested,

00:16:05.220 --> 00:16:07.560
we have an
opportunity to improve

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:11.340
on how we access legacy data
and documents to support

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:13.500
our decisions now.

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:15.170
So now let's take
a look at how we

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:20.370
have addressed this
challenge with tools on GCP.

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:23.880
In order to dig deeper into
the content of our documents,

00:16:23.880 --> 00:16:26.640
we first need to break them
down into the parts that can

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.010
be analyzed in a custom way.

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:32.700
This requires each
document to be segmented

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:36.510
and then these segments are
classified into either text

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:38.440
tables or images.

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:41.940
And then we have to order them
in order to holistically put

00:16:41.940 --> 00:16:44.460
them into context.

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:48.660
When a document is classified
as an image such as this one,

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:51.360
it's important to
extract the labels that

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:55.420
are within the image, as well
as the details within the image.

00:16:55.420 --> 00:16:58.320
So in this case, we
have a geologic model.

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:01.230
For some applications,
it's important for us

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:05.174
to understand those pieces as
well as the whole image itself.

00:17:08.569 --> 00:17:11.000
It's also important that
we capture that information

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:15.170
within tables and to
transform these tables which

00:17:15.170 --> 00:17:19.550
in documents are in an
unstructured data format,

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:24.500
we want to put these into
a structured data format

00:17:24.500 --> 00:17:28.400
so that they can be used
by our analysis tools

00:17:28.400 --> 00:17:31.310
in a much more efficient way.

00:17:31.310 --> 00:17:34.730
Our documents contain a
variety of image types

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:37.880
that are specific to the
geosciences, many of which

00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:39.500
that you can see here.

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:43.730
These range from seismic
data to earth models,

00:17:43.730 --> 00:17:47.270
to geologic maps, and much more.

00:17:47.270 --> 00:17:50.960
Some of you may
remember from Next 18

00:17:50.960 --> 00:17:54.110
that we used AutoML Vision
to classify these image.

00:17:54.110 --> 00:17:58.070
With AutoML, it's
straightforward to customize

00:17:58.070 --> 00:18:03.260
the classification scheme to
our Earth model content or Earth

00:18:03.260 --> 00:18:04.970
science content.

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:10.700
We simply upload hundreds
of images that we have,

00:18:10.700 --> 00:18:14.810
and then we upload
hundreds of images,

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:19.580
and then we label them with
some of the image labels

00:18:19.580 --> 00:18:21.690
that you see here.

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:24.050
And then, within a
matter of minutes,

00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:26.810
we can already have
an initial model

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:34.760
that can help us train our
model to classify everything

00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:36.350
that we have on our system.

00:18:36.350 --> 00:18:39.260
And then this can be
used to make that content

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:42.320
searchable further.

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:47.280
The AutoML interface
is very easy to use.

00:18:47.280 --> 00:18:52.190
There are very
simple tools that are

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:56.600
shown to the user, such as
the AutoML-generated confusion

00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:58.520
matrix right here.

00:18:58.520 --> 00:19:01.040
This is a very
straightforward way

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:04.610
that and an expert
in the geosciences

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:08.540
can understand where maybe they
may need to add more training

00:19:08.540 --> 00:19:13.820
examples to improve the model or
make other changes to increase

00:19:13.820 --> 00:19:14.990
the accuracy.

00:19:14.990 --> 00:19:16.760
In some cases,
maybe they just need

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:21.040
to train the model longer in
order to get a better result.

00:19:21.040 --> 00:19:23.660
But these types of
outputs are very

00:19:23.660 --> 00:19:29.360
easy for just normal subject
matter experts in subjects

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:30.770
other than machine learning.

00:19:30.770 --> 00:19:33.890
So technical experts to use.

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:38.280
And that's what we find
very useful about AutoML.

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:41.540
So as you can see
from the diagram here,

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:44.240
AutoML is not all that
we needed in order

00:19:44.240 --> 00:19:47.510
to make this work to make
everything searchable,

00:19:47.510 --> 00:19:50.030
so I'm going to call up
Kenton Prindle to talk

00:19:50.030 --> 00:19:51.930
about this in more detail.

00:19:51.930 --> 00:19:53.630
And for the last
couple of years,

00:19:53.630 --> 00:19:56.000
we've had a great
partnership with Google

00:19:56.000 --> 00:19:59.480
and he has helped us implement
this within our company.

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:02.510
And take it away, Kenton.

00:20:02.510 --> 00:20:04.140
KENTON PRINDLE:
Thank you, Laura.

00:20:04.140 --> 00:20:04.640
Oh, yes.

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:05.690
Thank you.

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:06.320
So, yes.

00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:08.278
As Laura mentioned, we've
been working together

00:20:08.278 --> 00:20:11.060
for quite a while now
on how to characterize

00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:13.220
these documents at a
meta level and then also

00:20:13.220 --> 00:20:14.970
at the segment level.

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:18.110
So as you can see on
the left-hand side--

00:20:18.110 --> 00:20:20.010
first, I mentioned
that, of course,

00:20:20.010 --> 00:20:22.340
this was last year's
information that we basically

00:20:22.340 --> 00:20:24.320
built with Chevron.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:26.750
And essentially, we're moving
past that quite aggressively

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:28.970
now to supplement
segmentation, as she

00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:31.310
mentioned before,
but also well logs,

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:34.020
LES files, and segue files.

00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:36.710
And our focus here is
actually connect it altogether

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:38.150
for true analog search.

00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:40.167
And so what we're
after is stuff that we

00:20:40.167 --> 00:20:41.750
can get greater and
greater and deeper

00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:43.450
insights into the
world's dataset,

00:20:43.450 --> 00:20:45.440
not just data assets dataset.

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:48.350
So on the left-hand side, you'll
see that the ingestion process

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:51.003
is just simply just
triggered by emptying

00:20:51.003 --> 00:20:52.670
your files into a
bucket, and the bucket

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:54.560
triggers a whole
series of events that

00:20:54.560 --> 00:20:57.420
automatically process the data.

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:59.600
So it goes to GSC
storage, Pub/Sub

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:02.840
handles all these events,
and moves to Cloud Dataflow.

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:05.060
Dataflow is only tasked
with one purpose, which

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:06.640
is to break it into pages.

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:09.685
The pages then can be
paralyzed for processing

00:21:09.685 --> 00:21:11.060
in the segmentation
space, right?

00:21:11.060 --> 00:21:13.490
So we go into the page,
we segment the page

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:15.230
as a human readable flow, right?

00:21:15.230 --> 00:21:18.380
So we keep all the text,
we keep all the images

00:21:18.380 --> 00:21:19.380
in the right order.

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:22.220
So that it was as if a human
was reading the document.

00:21:22.220 --> 00:21:25.070
Later in life, this would
become evident of why.

00:21:25.070 --> 00:21:27.178
It takes a long time to
do this, but we want not

00:21:27.178 --> 00:21:29.720
only the semantic expressions
of how the document information

00:21:29.720 --> 00:21:31.618
flows, we want sentiment.

00:21:31.618 --> 00:21:33.410
And sentiment is very,
very hard to achieve

00:21:33.410 --> 00:21:37.580
without keeping the word flow
of the entire vector space

00:21:37.580 --> 00:21:38.250
consistent.

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:40.220
So we're doing this
for some reason

00:21:40.220 --> 00:21:42.408
later, which we'll talk about.

00:21:42.408 --> 00:21:44.450
And then, of course, once
we get this information

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:46.367
on the second level,
we're actually passing it

00:21:46.367 --> 00:21:48.450
through a whole series of APIs.

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:52.510
So these APIs are, of
course, AutoML Vision, NLP,

00:21:52.510 --> 00:21:54.710
and Translation
amongst a few others.

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:56.150
And the whole
point here is we're

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:59.660
trying to figure out, with
every one of these holes,

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:04.250
whether they're an image, a
table, an equation, a drilling

00:22:04.250 --> 00:22:07.350
diagram, a table,
whatever it is, text.

00:22:07.350 --> 00:22:09.410
And so once we get
this, we actually

00:22:09.410 --> 00:22:11.780
run that adaptively
through the algorithm

00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:13.970
to figure out the information
we want off of it.

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:17.870
Once we do that, we actually
pass that into a Cloud Machine

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:20.690
Learning Engine called to
figure out a word to back,

00:22:20.690 --> 00:22:22.890
an image to bacl
encoded dual model.

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:23.390
Right?

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:24.920
And so in this
case, we're trying

00:22:24.920 --> 00:22:26.878
to figure out what the
type of the document is.

00:22:26.878 --> 00:22:29.360
So we're not stopping it, just
getting the information out

00:22:29.360 --> 00:22:31.160
for just general keyword search.

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:33.410
We're trying, then, to
go deeper and say, well,

00:22:33.410 --> 00:22:36.140
this is a drilling report
or this is a composite log.

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:38.180
Whatever it is, we're
trying to make it easy

00:22:38.180 --> 00:22:41.150
so you can do filter search
as well as just true vector

00:22:41.150 --> 00:22:43.100
search.

00:22:43.100 --> 00:22:45.020
From that point, Pub/Sub
delivers that back

00:22:45.020 --> 00:22:49.640
to the GCS archive, and all
of the segments of interest

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:53.210
are saved there for display.

00:22:53.210 --> 00:22:55.040
And then, of course,
we index those either

00:22:55.040 --> 00:22:56.900
to Cloud Search
or Elasticsearch.

00:22:56.900 --> 00:22:58.400
Cloud Search is our
preferred method

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:00.500
because we can scale much
more, but Elasticsearch

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:01.868
works perfect, as well.

00:23:01.868 --> 00:23:03.410
And then, of course,
we've integrated

00:23:03.410 --> 00:23:07.370
dialog flows that you can
talk to your interface.

00:23:07.370 --> 00:23:10.640
But that's not a
big focus of this.

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:12.710
Ultimately, what we end
up with is the documents

00:23:12.710 --> 00:23:14.870
on a map with the
ability to search images

00:23:14.870 --> 00:23:16.550
or text, or images and text.

00:23:19.790 --> 00:23:20.290
All right.

00:23:20.290 --> 00:23:22.210
So why are we doing all this?

00:23:22.210 --> 00:23:24.622
We're not just doing it
to pull up the images

00:23:24.622 --> 00:23:26.080
and pull up this
information, we're

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:27.850
trying to integrate
all the data, right?

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:29.350
And so on the top
you'll see that we

00:23:29.350 --> 00:23:32.090
have classic kind of digital
data that we experience a lot,

00:23:32.090 --> 00:23:36.100
we see well logs and
seismic, Earth models.

00:23:36.100 --> 00:23:40.480
These things are reasonably
well formatted and understood.

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:43.240
And then we're trying to
take the glue between those

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:45.375
and the interpretation
or the documents, right?

00:23:45.375 --> 00:23:46.750
They are the true
interpretations

00:23:46.750 --> 00:23:49.550
that we're actually getting
from these upper images.

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:52.000
So what we've done
recently with Chevron

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:53.650
is we've built a
knowledge graph that

00:23:53.650 --> 00:23:57.460
can handle the entity
resolution and geolocate

00:23:57.460 --> 00:23:59.020
based on that resolution.

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:03.440
So at this point, we've got LES
curves, we've got segue files,

00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:06.950
we've got any kind of attribute
of any of those feature sets.

00:24:06.950 --> 00:24:09.070
And we've got the documents
tied to those features

00:24:09.070 --> 00:24:14.887
in a connected similarity in
that 3D space, such as this.

00:24:14.887 --> 00:24:17.470
So you see here on the left, we
have this well log in seismic.

00:24:17.470 --> 00:24:19.720
It's pretty obvious when you
do this as a geophysicist

00:24:19.720 --> 00:24:22.973
that this well log belongs
to this piece of seismic.

00:24:22.973 --> 00:24:24.640
That's the classic
kind of view of this.

00:24:24.640 --> 00:24:27.287
But when you don't actually
have this stuff into a project,

00:24:27.287 --> 00:24:28.870
it's actually quite
hard to understand

00:24:28.870 --> 00:24:30.230
where the state is connected.

00:24:30.230 --> 00:24:32.480
So on the right-hand tops,
you see the Knowledge Graph

00:24:32.480 --> 00:24:33.520
representation of this.

00:24:33.520 --> 00:24:35.770
And you'll see that we'd
have nice Knowledge Graph

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:38.950
nodes connecting who
is the operator, what

00:24:38.950 --> 00:24:42.070
did this log and or
field from the document--

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:43.390
was it an oil producing field?

00:24:43.390 --> 00:24:44.740
Was it the Scott field?

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:47.320
And the point here is, now, when
you search for these things,

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:50.650
you're getting back the analog
system, not just the data

00:24:50.650 --> 00:24:52.060
that you're looking for.

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:53.710
We get all the
features that belong

00:24:53.710 --> 00:24:55.420
to that particular
search term if you're

00:24:55.420 --> 00:24:57.000
looking for a finite object.

00:24:57.000 --> 00:24:58.750
But then if you look
for a general object,

00:24:58.750 --> 00:25:02.090
like a porous sandstone of
north-west Shell Australia,

00:25:02.090 --> 00:25:04.720
for example, you actually
might light up a world

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:07.300
map and say here all
the Triassic sounds

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:09.100
that actually have
relevant features that

00:25:09.100 --> 00:25:10.257
are similar to this.

00:25:10.257 --> 00:25:11.965
And that's kind of
our goal in the space.

00:25:14.777 --> 00:25:15.360
Back to Laura.

00:25:21.678 --> 00:25:22.720
LAURA BANDURA: All right.

