00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:34.020
Pub/Sub in particular, we
had the utility management

00:28:34.020 --> 00:28:35.950
activity.

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:38.280
The electrical grid,
while it's been around

00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:41.100
for over 100 years
in some locations,

00:28:41.100 --> 00:28:43.780
we're constantly
doing work on it.

00:28:43.780 --> 00:28:47.340
We're changing the quality,
we're doing maintenance,

00:28:47.340 --> 00:28:50.760
we're doing repairs, and
so I want that information

00:28:50.760 --> 00:28:53.430
as soon as we can get it.

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:55.650
Also, we've got vegetation
management activity

00:28:55.650 --> 00:28:57.400
that happens every single day.

00:28:57.400 --> 00:29:01.160
We've got crews out
trimming trees, looking

00:29:01.160 --> 00:29:04.830
at the condition in the right
of way, looking at the condition

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:06.940
adjacent to the right of way.

00:29:06.940 --> 00:29:08.862
And then, last, I've
got the weather forecast

00:29:08.862 --> 00:29:10.320
because I want to
know what's going

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:12.570
to happen tomorrow and the
day after that and the week

00:29:12.570 --> 00:29:14.040
after that.

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:15.630
So we can ingest all of that.

00:29:15.630 --> 00:29:18.030
Obviously, we'd have
a tremendous amount

00:29:18.030 --> 00:29:20.310
of time series
processing we had to do.

00:29:20.310 --> 00:29:23.130
Spun up Dataproc,
was great for that.

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:27.120
We were storing results, both
base data and processed data,

00:29:27.120 --> 00:29:28.510
in cloud storage.

00:29:28.510 --> 00:29:30.510
And then we had a couple
of different approaches

00:29:30.510 --> 00:29:33.480
to our modeling that
evolved over time.

00:29:33.480 --> 00:29:36.120
We had the CloudML
Engine, where were running

00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:38.280
some of our models,
and then also we

00:29:38.280 --> 00:29:39.660
just used the Cloud Datalab.

00:29:39.660 --> 00:29:42.180
It was great for just
getting quick answers.

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:44.370
And then we would read all
that out into a front end

00:29:44.370 --> 00:29:48.600
because, as a data scientist,
I was really happy back here.

00:29:48.600 --> 00:29:51.330
But for my business
users, I needed to publish

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:54.333
and it was great that I could
put results into Cloud SQL

00:29:54.333 --> 00:29:56.250
because then they could
feed it into all kinds

00:29:56.250 --> 00:29:58.990
of downstream applications.

00:29:58.990 --> 00:30:01.620
And then I could put a web
UI on the front, right?

00:30:01.620 --> 00:30:04.260
I didn't know who all
the users might be,

00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:07.355
but I knew that I could make it
available to them on the Google

00:30:07.355 --> 00:30:07.980
Cloud Platform.

00:30:11.550 --> 00:30:14.760
This, for us, was very much
a journey of discovery.

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:17.050
We did not sit down in a
conference room and say,

00:30:17.050 --> 00:30:19.942
based on everything we know,
let's architect this thing once

00:30:19.942 --> 00:30:21.650
and I'm sure we're
going to get it right.

00:30:21.650 --> 00:30:23.442
And I think you may
have heard some of that

00:30:23.442 --> 00:30:25.505
and what Kenton
talked about as well.

00:30:25.505 --> 00:30:26.880
So a couple of
things we learned,

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:28.505
I wanted to share
with you, because you

00:30:28.505 --> 00:30:30.240
may go on a similar journey.

00:30:30.240 --> 00:30:32.490
One on the left,
the architecture.

00:30:32.490 --> 00:30:34.312
We sat down with
the initial design.

00:30:34.312 --> 00:30:36.270
Our feeling was, this is
probably a good start,

00:30:36.270 --> 00:30:37.840
we got started.

00:30:37.840 --> 00:30:40.752
And we learned, we didn't
nail it the first time.

00:30:40.752 --> 00:30:42.460
But there are some
great tools out there.

00:30:42.460 --> 00:30:46.110
In particular, the official
icons and the sample diagrams

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:48.900
was great as we could bring
in subject matter experts

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:51.250
and talk about the problem,
talk about the data,

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:55.200
talk about the challenges we had
and some of the ETL processes

00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:57.390
and say, does this
feel like something

00:30:57.390 --> 00:30:59.370
that's been solved before?

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:01.650
Does Google already have
tools and accelerators

00:31:01.650 --> 00:31:02.400
that we could use?

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:05.570
And the answer was yes.

00:31:05.570 --> 00:31:07.310
Also, on the pricing.

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:09.230
I'm in a professional
services organization.

00:31:09.230 --> 00:31:12.840
People always ask me, how
long is it going to take?

00:31:12.840 --> 00:31:14.730
What's it going to cost?

00:31:14.730 --> 00:31:17.720
And so some of the pricing
wasn't known, right?

00:31:17.720 --> 00:31:20.540
The answer is, well, depends
how fast you want to go?

00:31:20.540 --> 00:31:22.200
How soon do you want it?

00:31:22.200 --> 00:31:23.347
So we had some great--

00:31:23.347 --> 00:31:25.430
we used the pricing
calculators that worked really

00:31:25.430 --> 00:31:29.060
well because I could then put
that back into my client's hand

00:31:29.060 --> 00:31:31.560
and say, it's your decision.

00:31:31.560 --> 00:31:32.630
The tools are here.

00:31:32.630 --> 00:31:34.760
They will support you to do it.

00:31:34.760 --> 00:31:37.190
It's really what's
your budget and what's

00:31:37.190 --> 00:31:40.400
your demand in the market.

00:31:40.400 --> 00:31:43.380
Also, the inexperience
with the new tools.

00:31:43.380 --> 00:31:46.730
The Google Cloud Platform
continues to evolve.

00:31:46.730 --> 00:31:47.510
You saw today.

00:31:47.510 --> 00:31:50.660
We've got new tools
available to us.

00:31:50.660 --> 00:31:52.820
There's also been an
evolution of the tools

00:31:52.820 --> 00:31:55.310
we were using while
we were using them.

00:31:55.310 --> 00:31:57.680
And that worked really
well, but it meant

00:31:57.680 --> 00:31:59.160
we weren't always experienced.

00:31:59.160 --> 00:32:03.410
So I could take junior
folks and get them involved,

00:32:03.410 --> 00:32:05.960
build on what they already
knew, and then get them

00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:08.520
deeper into the Google
Cloud ecosystem.

00:32:08.520 --> 00:32:10.910
So that was really great for
building morale in the team

00:32:10.910 --> 00:32:13.625
and sustaining the
energy on this project.

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:18.100
We also dealt a lot
with the complexity

00:32:18.100 --> 00:32:20.140
in building the data
science pipelines.

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:23.900
There is a lot of
math, it's complex.

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:29.200
But what I've found is I could
tell people, just go try it,

00:32:29.200 --> 00:32:30.070
just get started.

00:32:30.070 --> 00:32:32.467
I could put the
tools in their hands

00:32:32.467 --> 00:32:33.550
and it worked really well.

00:32:33.550 --> 00:32:36.310
They could do prototyping,
they could get into Sheets,

00:32:36.310 --> 00:32:38.020
we could say, here's
what I want to do,

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:39.730
now I want to do it even bigger.

00:32:39.730 --> 00:32:43.240
I'm kind of excited about
the AutoML work and some

00:32:43.240 --> 00:32:45.430
of the connected Sheet
stuff we saw today.

00:32:45.430 --> 00:32:49.680
I might be using
that by next week.

00:32:49.680 --> 00:32:51.310
And then, the other
thing is, because I

00:32:51.310 --> 00:32:55.930
had a smaller, very high-trust
team, it was great for me.

00:32:55.930 --> 00:32:59.320
I had great visibility
and my whole team

00:32:59.320 --> 00:33:01.102
had visibility to
each other's work.

00:33:01.102 --> 00:33:02.560
And so that made
it very efficient.

00:33:02.560 --> 00:33:05.710
If somebody was out or they
had a question how it was done,

00:33:05.710 --> 00:33:07.330
I could always get
to the repository,

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:09.790
I could see the work, I
could see the workflow.

00:33:09.790 --> 00:33:12.640
And if my leadership wanted
to audit and understand

00:33:12.640 --> 00:33:14.830
or my client wanted
to dig deeper,

00:33:14.830 --> 00:33:16.420
it's all there for them to see.

00:33:16.420 --> 00:33:18.640
It's transparent.

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:21.360
And then, the last
part, the DevOps basics.

00:33:21.360 --> 00:33:24.200
This stuff will kill you if
you don't think about it.

00:33:24.200 --> 00:33:25.510
It's a lot harder.

00:33:25.510 --> 00:33:29.150
If you did it the old way, it
took a lot of time and money.

00:33:29.150 --> 00:33:30.590
So I was very excited.

00:33:30.590 --> 00:33:34.720
We felt that natively building
these applications in GCP

00:33:34.720 --> 00:33:36.340
cut out a lot of time and cost.

00:33:40.390 --> 00:33:43.820
For those of you who
like the pretty pictures,

00:33:43.820 --> 00:33:47.780
here's an example on your left
of one of our early models.

00:33:47.780 --> 00:33:50.390
You can see some of
the utility footprint,

00:33:50.390 --> 00:33:53.500
you can see some of the trees,
some of the risk factors

00:33:53.500 --> 00:33:57.500
are modeled out in the grid.

00:33:57.500 --> 00:33:59.030
And that was great.

00:33:59.030 --> 00:34:00.820
We got an answer.

00:34:00.820 --> 00:34:05.500
But, of course, that very
day we read out the results

00:34:05.500 --> 00:34:06.220
we've got it.

00:34:06.220 --> 00:34:08.080
I think we've got it.

00:34:08.080 --> 00:34:11.830
I got asked, well,
hey, this is great.

00:34:11.830 --> 00:34:13.630
Can we expand the spatial scope?

00:34:13.630 --> 00:34:15.219
Can we build out
to adjacent states?

00:34:15.219 --> 00:34:17.239
Can we go to other
utilities and territories,

00:34:17.239 --> 00:34:21.360
because we trade power with them
and if they go down I go down?

00:34:21.360 --> 00:34:24.489
We got asked for
expanded temporal scope.

00:34:24.489 --> 00:34:26.750
Can you take more time?

00:34:26.750 --> 00:34:28.589
How about could we
add more variables?

00:34:28.589 --> 00:34:30.070
Oh, I've got a
really great idea,

00:34:30.070 --> 00:34:31.929
I met somebody who's
in forestry and he

00:34:31.929 --> 00:34:34.840
was explaining to me the physics
and how the different species

00:34:34.840 --> 00:34:37.000
of trees have different
elasticities in the wind,

00:34:37.000 --> 00:34:40.120
like, hey, yes.

00:34:40.120 --> 00:34:42.580
We're on a platform
where we can do that.

00:34:42.580 --> 00:34:45.100
Really what we're left with
are our business priorities.

00:34:45.100 --> 00:34:48.600
Which one do you want
to bite off first?

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:50.770
That allowed us to run
lots of model variants

00:34:50.770 --> 00:34:54.520
as well to explore to the extent
the research was not clear

00:34:54.520 --> 00:34:56.500
and there were
opportunities for us

00:34:56.500 --> 00:34:58.990
to work and expand on
some of the research

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:01.160
that the universities had done.

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:02.710
We were able to do that.

00:35:02.710 --> 00:35:06.550
And, ultimately, I'm
a business person.

00:35:06.550 --> 00:35:07.750
Could we make it actionable?

00:35:07.750 --> 00:35:10.720
Could we make it easier for
the downstream applications

00:35:10.720 --> 00:35:13.900
to consume this information
and change the work orders that

00:35:13.900 --> 00:35:18.090
go out tomorrow to actually make
a difference out in the world?

00:35:20.595 --> 00:35:22.470
The other piece of this
is really interesting

00:35:22.470 --> 00:35:25.980
if you come at it from
a policy perspective.

00:35:25.980 --> 00:35:27.832
So we put together the
model that already--

00:35:27.832 --> 00:35:29.790
let's talk about the
accuracy and the precision

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:30.780
and the recall.

00:35:30.780 --> 00:35:33.240
Let's get into the numbers.

00:35:33.240 --> 00:35:35.250
And that's, of
course, important.

00:35:35.250 --> 00:35:38.590
Obviously along the diagonal
here, the true positives,

00:35:38.590 --> 00:35:39.090
right?

00:35:39.090 --> 00:35:41.100
The ones we got right.

00:35:41.100 --> 00:35:43.130
Also important,
the true negatives.

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:45.780
We predicted no fire,
there was no fire.

00:35:45.780 --> 00:35:47.910
But let's talk about
the off diagonal.

00:35:47.910 --> 00:35:50.540
This is really interesting
policy discussion.

00:35:50.540 --> 00:35:53.610
In the bottom left
corner, we predicted

00:35:53.610 --> 00:35:55.440
there would be a fire.

00:35:55.440 --> 00:35:57.630
But in fact, there wasn't any.

00:35:57.630 --> 00:35:58.500
So what's the harm?

00:35:58.500 --> 00:36:01.320
What have we done when
we're wrong in this model?

00:36:01.320 --> 00:36:04.140
I've sent somebody
out to trim trees.

00:36:04.140 --> 00:36:06.210
Well, the utility
is obligated to go

00:36:06.210 --> 00:36:08.890
through the whole
territory every year,

00:36:08.890 --> 00:36:10.410
every two years,
every four years.

00:36:10.410 --> 00:36:11.910
It depends on the
risk and where you

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:16.710
are with the utility, what the
regulation is in your state.

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:20.370
But, really, I've maybe
accelerated when we got to it.

00:36:20.370 --> 00:36:22.190
But nobody's been harmed.

00:36:22.190 --> 00:36:24.540
It's just relatively
low cost for something

00:36:24.540 --> 00:36:28.170
I was going to do
operationally anyway.

00:36:28.170 --> 00:36:31.000
The top rate is the one
you don't want to be wrong.

00:36:31.000 --> 00:36:35.350
That's when we say, I don't
think that's the dangerous one,

00:36:35.350 --> 00:36:37.030
but it turns out it was.

00:36:37.030 --> 00:36:40.185
So when we got into all the
hyperparameter tuning, that's

00:36:40.185 --> 00:36:42.310
the one where you said, I
got to make that thing as

00:36:42.310 --> 00:36:44.200
small as possible.

00:36:44.200 --> 00:36:46.690
Every data set you can give
me, every transformation you

00:36:46.690 --> 00:36:50.650
can think of, and I was never
limited by the platform.

00:36:50.650 --> 00:36:54.790
I'm limited by our imagination,
by the research, and by time.

00:36:57.660 --> 00:36:59.867
Some examples, then, we
also found was helpful.

00:36:59.867 --> 00:37:02.450
You got great big data sets and
want to know, so where were we

00:37:02.450 --> 00:37:02.950
right?

00:37:02.950 --> 00:37:04.400
Where were we wrong?

00:37:04.400 --> 00:37:06.855
Two examples up here,
false positives.

00:37:06.855 --> 00:37:08.480
These are actually
kind of a fun story.

00:37:08.480 --> 00:37:12.560
I was wrong because
we got there early.

00:37:12.560 --> 00:37:14.870
I told you 12 hours
before it failed,

00:37:14.870 --> 00:37:17.070
I thought it was going to fail.

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:20.270
So in my mind, that's
actually a success story.

00:37:20.270 --> 00:37:24.860
I was wrong, but the impact of
that is you were to cut it down

00:37:24.860 --> 00:37:26.770
and we'd all be a lot happier.

00:37:26.770 --> 00:37:29.360
But on the right-hand side,
some that we got right.

00:37:29.360 --> 00:37:32.502
Not only the location
and the time,

00:37:32.502 --> 00:37:33.710
we understood the conditions.

00:37:33.710 --> 00:37:36.458
And that made us feel
like we've got this.

00:37:36.458 --> 00:37:38.750
This is good enough that we
can start to operationalize

00:37:38.750 --> 00:37:41.870
and we can roll it out, we can
make decisions with this data,

00:37:41.870 --> 00:37:43.610
with this model with this team.

00:37:46.330 --> 00:37:49.220
So a couple of takeaways.

00:37:49.220 --> 00:37:52.750
First, we really had
a great experience

00:37:52.750 --> 00:37:54.760
with the Google Cloud Platform.

00:37:54.760 --> 00:37:57.890
It's an ecosystem,
it's scaled for us

00:37:57.890 --> 00:38:00.520
and allowed us to integrate,
and it allowed our product

00:38:00.520 --> 00:38:03.310
development team to get out
and come up with new ideas.

00:38:03.310 --> 00:38:07.030
And I never had to tell them
the technology doesn't do that.

00:38:07.030 --> 00:38:09.820
You'd say, yeah, we can do that.

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:13.910
And the second part is, there
are a lot of ways to get there.

00:38:13.910 --> 00:38:16.390
I showed you one of the
architectures that we

00:38:16.390 --> 00:38:19.430
implemented on this journey.

00:38:19.430 --> 00:38:22.720
But I would encourage
you to experiment.

00:38:22.720 --> 00:38:25.720
It's easy to set up, it's
easy to run through it,

00:38:25.720 --> 00:38:28.400
and it's easy to tear down
and try something different.

00:38:28.400 --> 00:38:30.790
Think of it as a
journey and think of it

00:38:30.790 --> 00:38:34.840
as one that's actually
kind of fun to go on.

00:38:34.840 --> 00:38:36.940
The other piece is,
for me, personally,

00:38:36.940 --> 00:38:39.760
that efficiency
and the accuracy--

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:40.850
that was so important.

00:38:40.850 --> 00:38:43.510
My feeling-- I could get my
team motivated every single day

00:38:43.510 --> 00:38:46.440
and say, today we're
going to make it better.

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:48.190
We're going to build
on everything we had.

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:49.000
It was all there.

00:38:49.000 --> 00:38:52.507
And we had a way to share those
learnings with each other.

00:38:52.507 --> 00:38:53.590
LUKMAN RAMSEY: Once again.

00:38:53.590 --> 00:38:56.710
Thank you very much for
attending this session

00:38:56.710 --> 00:38:59.050
and thank you again to
all of our presenters

00:38:59.050 --> 00:39:01.840
from Chevron,
Hitachi, and Nissen.

00:39:01.840 --> 00:39:02.710
Thank you.

00:39:02.710 --> 00:39:05.760
[MUSIC PLAYING]

